                  <div>
                    <div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:11.5px;letter-spacing:.12em;color:var(--faint);text-transform:uppercase">Case 02 · Software Engineer · Highpoint Growth Networks</div>
                    <h3 style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.4vw,36px);font-weight:600;margin:10px 0 0;letter-spacing:-.02em">Owning the billing data layer</h3>
                  </div>
                  <span style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:11px;color:var(--muted);border:1px solid var(--line-2);border-radius:6px;padding:5px 9px">US · AD-TECH SAAS</span>
                </div>
                <div class="pdr-grid" style="background:var(--line);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;overflow:hidden;margin-top:26px">
                  <div style="background:var(--bg-soft);padding:15px 18px">
                    <div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.16em;color:var(--faint);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:10px">Problem</div>
                    <p style="margin:0;font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--muted)">Billing and subscription data for Highpoint's customers lived entirely inside a third-party billing provider's own application — Captiview (Highpoint's ad-tech platform) had no direct ownership or visibility into that data. As the platform scaled, this became a growing dependency: critical customer records existed only in a third-party interface, disconnected from the product Captiview was actually building.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div style="background:var(--bg-soft);padding:15px 18px">
                    <div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.16em;color:var(--faint);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:10px">Technical decision</div>
                    <p style="margin:0;font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ink)">Architected an integration with the external payment processor's API to individualize and centralize billing records directly inside Captiview, rather than relying on that provider's application as the source of truth. The migration moved billing ownership in-house: Captiview became the system of record for every subscription, syncing full billing history for all existing customers into its own database instead of depending on a third-party interface.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div style="background:var(--bg-soft);padding:15px 18px">
                    <div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.16em;color:var(--accent);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:10px">Result</div>
                    <p style="margin:0;font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ink)">Hundreds of active subscriptions integrated with zero disruption to existing customers. Captiview now owns and controls its subscription data instead of depending solely on the third-party provider's interface.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div style="margin-top:30px;padding-top:28px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)">
